.TH TIME 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
time \- run a program and report how long it took
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B time
.RB [ \-pa ]
.RB [ \-f
.IR fmt ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR file ]
.I prog
.RI [ arg ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B time
runs
.I prog
with the arguments
.IR arg ,
waits for it to exit, and writes a report of its elapsed time and the CPU
time it used.
.I prog
shares the standard input, standard output, and standard error of
.BR time .
The report goes to standard error, or with
.B \-o
to a file, and
.B time
exits with the status of
.IR prog .
.PP
.I prog
is found as for any command that busybox runs; see
.B Running commands
in
.BR busybox (1).
.SS Report
The default report has three lines, with a tab after each label:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
real	0m 1.02s
user	0m 0.01s
sys	0m 0.00s
.EE
.RE
.PP
.B real
is the elapsed time, and
.B user
and
.B sys
are the time that
.I prog
spent in user mode and in the kernel.
Times are truncated to hundredths of a second.
With
.BR \-p ,
the times are in seconds and a space follows each label:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
real 1.02
user 0.01
sys 0.00
.EE
.RE
.PP
If
.I prog
exits with a nonzero status, or is terminated by a signal, one more line
comes first, in the same place as the report and for every format:
.B Command exited with non-zero status
.I n
or
.B Command terminated by signal
.IR n .
.SS Format
The format of
.B \-f
and
.B TIME
is text in which a percent sign starts a directive and a backslash starts
an escape.
Other text is written as it is, and
.B time
ends the report with a newline.
The directives are:
.TP
.B %%
A percent sign.
.TP
.B %C
.I prog
and its arguments, separated by single spaces, as
.B time
received them and without quoting.
.TP
.B %E
The elapsed time as minutes and seconds with hundredths, such as
.BR "0m 1.02s" .
From one hour on, it is hours, minutes, and whole seconds, such as
.BR "1h 2m 03s" .
.TP
.B %e
The elapsed time in seconds with hundredths, without minutes, such as
.B 1.02
or
.BR 125.50 .
.TP
.B %S
The system CPU time in seconds with hundredths.
.TP
.B %T
The system CPU time in the form of
.BR %E .
.TP
.B %U
The user CPU time in seconds with hundredths.
.TP
.B %u
The user CPU time in the form of
.BR %E .
.TP
.B %x
The exit status of
.I prog
in decimal, or 0 if a signal terminated it.
.PP
Any other character after a percent sign is written as a question mark
followed by that character, so
.B %P
gives
.BR ?P .
A format that ends in a lone percent sign ends the report with a
question mark and no newline.
.PP
The escapes are
.B \en
for a newline,
.B \et
for a tab, and
.B \e\e
for a backslash.
Any other character after a backslash is written as a question mark, a
backslash, and that character.
A format that ends in a lone backslash ends the report with a question
mark, a backslash,
.I prog
as given, and a newline.
.PP
An empty format writes only a newline.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-pa ,
and an option's argument may be attached, as in
.BR \-f%e ,
or be the next argument.
Option parsing stops at the first operand, which is
.IR prog :
everything after it belongs to
.IR prog ,
even an argument that looks like an option.
.B \-\-
ends the options.
At least one operand is required.
.TP
.B \-p
Use the POSIX report, shown above.
It overrides
.B \-f
and
.BR TIME .
.TP
.BI "\-f " fmt
Use
.I fmt
as the format of the report; see
.BR Format .
It overrides
.BR TIME .
.TP
.BI "\-o " file
Write the report to
.I file
instead of standard error.
.B time
creates
.I file
if it does not exist, and truncates it unless
.B \-a
is given.
It opens
.I file
before it starts
.IR prog ,
so the file exists even if
.I prog
cannot be started.
.TP
.B \-a
Append to the
.B \-o
file instead of truncating it.
It has no effect without
.BR \-o .
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit with status 0.
It is recognized only as the first argument, and any further arguments
are ignored.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
busybox expands wildcards in the arguments of
.B time
itself when it starts, which includes those meant for
.IR prog ,
as needed when run from
.BR cmd.exe .
If the variable is not set then, busybox sets it to
.BR 0 ,
so
.IR prog 's
environment has
.BR BB_GLOBBING=0 .
.TP
.B BB_OVERRIDE_APPLETS
Says which names run an applet and which run a program from
.B PATH
when
.I prog
is looked up; see
.BR busybox (1).
.TP
.B PATH
Where
.I prog
is looked up when it is not an applet and its name has no directory.
Directories are separated by semicolons.
.TP
.B TIME
The format of the report, when neither
.B \-f
nor
.B \-p
is given.
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
.I prog
exited with status 0, or
.B \-\-help
printed the usage message.
.TP
.B 1
An invalid option, an option without its argument, or no
.I prog
operand, with a usage message on standard error.
Also the
.B \-o
file could not be opened, or
.I prog
could not be started.
.PP
For the last two, standard error gets one of
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
time: can\(aqt open \(aq\fIfile\fR\(aq: \fIreason\fR
time: can\(aqt execute \fIprog\fR: \fIreason\fR
.EE
.RE
.PP
Otherwise the status is that of
.IR prog ,
which may also be 1.
If a signal terminated
.IR prog ,
it is the signal number, not 128 plus it; see
.BR NOTES .
.SH EXAMPLES
Time a command:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ time sleep 1
real	0m 1.02s
user	0m 0.01s
sys	0m 0.00s
.EE
.RE
.PP
Use the POSIX format:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ time \-p sleep 1
real 1.02
user 0.00
sys 0.00
.EE
.RE
.PP
Print the command and its elapsed seconds:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ time \-f \(aq%C: %e seconds\(aq sleep 1
sleep 1: 1.01 seconds
.EE
.RE
.PP
Add a line per build to a log, apart from the build's own output:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ time \-a \-o build.log \-f \(aq%C %E\(aq make
.EE
.RE
.PP
Time a pipeline.
Only the elapsed time is meaningful, because the work is done in
processes started by
.BR sh :
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ time \-f %e sh \-c \(aqsort big.txt | uniq \-c >counts.txt\(aq
.EE
.RE
.PP
Choose a format for every run in a shell:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ export TIME=\(aq%C took %E\(aq
$ time sleep 1
sleep 1 took 0m 1.01s
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
.B time
reads the CPU times of
.I prog
from Windows once it exits.
They are the times of that one process: unlike on Unix, the processes it
starts are not counted, even those it waits for.
For a script, they are those of its interpreter, and for a batch file
those of
.BR cmd.exe ,
not of the programs they run.
When
.I prog
is an empty batch file,
.B time
prints
.BR "time: wait: Invalid argument" ,
and its report and exit status are meaningless.
.PP
The elapsed time comes from the system clock, not a monotonic clock, so
changing the clock while
.I prog
runs changes the result.
It is kept in 32 bits of milliseconds and wraps after about 49.7 days.
.PP
The Unix build of this applet also has
.B \-v
and the directives
.BR %c ,
.BR %D ,
.BR %F ,
.BR %I ,
.BR %K ,
.BR %k ,
.BR %M ,
.BR %O ,
.BR %P ,
.BR %p ,
.BR %R ,
.BR %r ,
.BR %s ,
.BR %t ,
.BR %W ,
.BR %w ,
.BR %X ,
and
.BR %Z ,
for the CPU percentage, memory use, page faults, and other counts.
This build has none of them:
.B \-v
is an invalid option, and each such directive is written as a question
mark and its letter.
.PP
Unlike the Unix build,
.B time
does not ignore Ctrl+C while it waits.
A Ctrl+C in the console ends
.B time
as well as
.IR prog ,
and no report is written.
.PP
The exit code of a Windows process has 32 bits.
Busybox treats some codes as signals:
.B 0xc0000005
(an access violation) is signal 11,
.B 0xc000013a
(Ctrl+C) is signal 2, and a code whose low three bytes are zero is the
signal in its top byte, when
.B kill \-l
lists that number.
The last is how busybox ends a process that it signals, as
.BR kill (1)
does.
For these,
.B time
exits with the signal number.
.PP
Any other code above 255, such as the NTSTATUS value of a crash, becomes
its low byte, or 255 when that is zero.
When it reduces a code,
.B time
first writes this to standard error, even when
.B \-o
is given:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
time: \fImessage\fR Error 0x\fIcode\fR
.EE
.RE
.PP
Here
.I message
is the system's description of the code, and
.I code
is in lowercase hexadecimal.
Without a description,
.I message
and the space after it are left out.
The reduced status is the one that
.B Command exited with non-zero status
and
.B %x
show.
.PP
.B time
is an ordinary command, and the kit's shell has no
.B time
keyword.
A shell builtin or function that is not also an applet cannot be timed:
.B time cd /
fails with status 1.
A pipeline after
.B time
belongs to the shell, so
.B time a | b
times only
.BR a .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR env (1),
.BR kill (1),
.BR sh (1),
.BR timeout (1)
